g("TDD / BDD", "method", "Test-Driven Development is the practice of writing the failing test before the code that makes it pass. Behavior-Driven Development writes the acceptance criteria as behaviors (given, when, then) that a test suite can check.", "Kent Beck, Test-Driven Development: By Example (2002); Dan North, 'Introducing BDD' (2006).", "The two practices are Gates 2 and 4 of the course's five gates.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 3", aliases=("TDD", "BDD", "pytest", "uv run pytest"), ch=(3, 19))
g("Golden set", "method", "A golden set is a fixed set of inputs with known-good outputs that the evaluation runs against every change. The regression delta is the difference between the baseline run and the candidate run, and the gate turns red when the delta crosses the threshold you pre-registered.", "", "E10 builds one (Figure 11), because one run is an anecdote and only a fixed set run twice gives a delta.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 19", aliases=("golden.jsonl", "regression delta", "delta gate", "baseline"), ch=(19,))
g("Red team", "method", "A red team probes its own system the way an adversary would and records what the system did. In this course the nine failure modes of Appendix C are the probe list.", "The term comes from military exercises in which a 'red' team plays the adversary.", "E11's lab plants falsehoods and injections and then reads the traces.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 20", aliases=("red-team", "Red Team", "adversary", "second-family adversary"), ch=(20,))
g("Lock with a TTL", "method", "A lock with a TTL is a mutual-exclusion token that expires. A worker that holds the lock on a file or a task must renew it before the Time To Live runs out or lose it, so a crashed worker cannot block the others forever.", "", "E9's build uses one to coordinate parallel workers.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 18", aliases=("TTL", "lock", "locks"), ch=(18,))
g("Rollback", "method", "A rollback returns a deployed system to its previous release, and the rollback itself is recorded in the ledger.", "", "E12 argues that the way back must be shown to work before the way forward is used.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 21", aliases=("rollback", "code freeze", "Release"), ch=(21,))
g("JSONL", "method", "JSON Lines is a text format with one JSON object per line, which makes a file easy to append to and to read one record at a time.", "", "The ledger, memory.jsonl, and golden.jsonl all use it.","https://jsonlines.org", aliases=("jsonl",))
g("SHA-256", "method", "SHA-256 is a cryptographic hash function. It turns a file or a string into a fixed-length fingerprint that changes if any byte of the input changes.", "NIST's SHA-2 family (2001).", "Receipts carry one, the golden set is pinned by one, and Figure 06's hash-pin check guards a tool description with one.","", aliases=("sha256", "hash", "hash-pin"))
g("Workflow versus agent", "method", "Anthropic's 2024 engineering post distinguishes a workflow, in which code fixes the sequence of model calls and tool calls in advance, from an agent, which decides its own next step inside a loop. The post lists five workflow patterns (prompt chaining, routing, parallelization, orchestrator-workers, evaluator-optimizer) before it reaches the agent.", "Anthropic, 'Building Effective Agents' (December 2024).", "E2 and Figure 03 use the distinction to ask who owns the loop, the code or the model.","https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents", aliases=("workflow", "workflows", "five patterns", "orchestrator-workers", "evaluator-optimizer"), ch=(11,))
g("Agentic AI", "method", "Agentic AI is the stage in the course's five-stage progression (foundation, creation, core systems, action, full automation) at which a model perceives, reasons, plans, and acts on an environment through tools, in a loop that has a halt condition. As the term is used in this book, an agent is a system whose reasoner is a language model and whose loop halts.", "", "E0 draws two maps, the seven-layer stack and the five-stage progression, and this term locates the course on the second.","Course Topic Guide, Chapters 1 and 2", aliases=("agent", "Agent", "agents", "halt", "halt condition", "Halt", "loop", "the loop", "agent loop"), ch=(1, 2))
g("LLM", "method", "A large language model is a transformer trained on text to predict the next token, and a program reaches it through a chat or completion API. It is the inner ring of Figure 17: one window goes in, one answer comes out, and nothing is kept between calls.", "The transformer architecture dates from Vaswani et al. (2017).", "Every agent in this course has a language model as its reasoner, so the rest of the vocabulary assumes this one.","", aliases=("language model", "model", "token", "tokens"))
g("SDK / API / CLI", "method", "A Software Development Kit is a library for building on a service (the Claude Agent SDK, the OpenAI Agents SDK, the MCP Python SDK). An API is the HTTP interface itself, and a CLI is a command-line program that a person runs in a terminal.", "", "The course treats an SDK as the harness's loop exposed to your code.","", aliases=("SDK", "SDKs", "API", "CLI"))

# ---------------- additions from the Oct 22 lecture-gap audit ----------------
g("OpenCode models: Big Pickle, Zen, Go", "harness", "In this course a model reaches OpenCode in one of three ways. Big Pickle is OpenCode's free model on the Zen gateway, a model of undisclosed family with an unpublished context window, offered at no charge with the caveat that data from the free period may be used for training. OpenCode Zen is the gateway's set of models whose families are disclosed. OpenCode Go is the paid tier ($10 a month as of fall 2026), with dollar limits per five hours, per week, and per month. Route B students use Ollama instead.", "", "Every exercise states which model it runs on, and the cost tables in E6 and E9 are computed against these tiers.","https://opencode.ai/docs/zen/; https://opencode.ai/docs/go/", aliases=("Big Pickle", "opencode/big-pickle", "OpenCode Zen", "OpenCode Go", "Zen gateway"))
g("System card", "paper", "A system card is the evaluation report a lab publishes with one model release. It states what was tested and how, the residual failure rates, the safety level the model ships under, and the behaviors seen in red-teaming.", "Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google DeepMind each publish them.", "Appendix C's nine failure modes are sourced to frontier system cards, and E11 reads two of them as Design Analyses.","https://www.anthropic.com/system-cards; https://openai.com/safety/", aliases=("system cards", "frontier system cards", "ASL-3"), ch=(20,))
g("Guardrail", "course", "A guardrail is a mechanism at an enforcement locus that refuses an action whatever the model has decided (a permission deny, a server-side scope check, a hook, a commit-time diff check). A sentence in a prompt asks the model to refrain and cannot itself refuse, so the course does not count it as a guardrail.", "", "E11's attack cards each state the guardrail under test, because the course's test of any safety claim is where the check runs and whether it can say no.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 20", aliases=("guardrails",), ch=(12, 20))
g("Enforcement locus", "course", "An enforcement locus is the place in the pipeline where a check runs and can refuse. The course distinguishes three: at the call (a schema or a hook), at the effect (a commit-time diff, git diff --cached), and at the door (release).", "gzkit moved its write fences from the call to the commit locus after the August 2026 hook incident, in which a hook never saw the write it was guarding.", "The term tells a reader of a guardrail where to look for the check, because a check that does not sit on the path of the write cannot refuse it.","Course Topic Guide, Chapters 12 and 20; Appendix G", aliases=("commit locus", "enforcement loci"), ch=(12, 20))
g("Incident casebook (Appendix G)", "course", "Appendix G is gzkit's dated record of its own failures from January to August 2026. It includes the first repudiated attestation (June 13), four briefs attested Completed for code that did not exist (June 16), the ten-deep subagent chain whose summaries became facts (May 24), the stale-clone handoff (April 26), the hook that never saw the write (August 21–23), and the marker-file gate (August 21).", "", "Every 'reference design' slide draws on it. The course argues that these failures are ordinary, and the argument rests on having the receipts for them.","Course Topic Guide, Appendix G", aliases=("Appendix G", "casebook", "incident casebook"), ch=(9,))
g("Marker-file gate (presence is not procedure)", "course", "In the gzkit incident of August 21, 2026, a pipeline gate checked only that a marker file was present, and a stale marker licensed about 350 lines of change with no dispatch and no review. The rule gzkit wrote afterward is that a presence check says 'armed' and never 'ran', so a gate must read the record of the run.", "", "The incident is the course's example of a gate that looks for a file rather than a result.","Course Topic Guide, Appendix G", aliases=("presence is not procedure", "marker file", "undispatched Stage 2"), ch=(9, 19))
g("Trust doctrine (T0–T3)", "course", "The trust doctrine is gzkit's assignment of a trust tier to every kind of input. T0 is the operator's own words, T1 is repository canon, T2 is tool and command output, and T3 is anything a model wrote or fetched. Trust-chain poisoning is the failure in which each layer accepts the layer below as fact, so that no single step is wrong and the conclusion at the top is.", "gzkit's April 18, 2026 outage is the casebook example of trust-chain poisoning.", "The DO IT RIGHT rules and the dispatch record both rest on it: a subagent's report is T3 until a human or a test confirms it.","https://gzkit.org/governance/trust-doctrine/", aliases=("trust doctrine", "trust-chain poisoning", "trust tier"), ch=(20,))
g("MX mode", "course", "MX mode is gzkit's maintenance posture after the June 2026 audit (ADR-0.0.74), stated as 'loose in the bay, hard at the door.' Work in progress may be untidy, and every gate holds at the release door. The MX hangar is the branch where that work happens.", "", "E12's release gates apply the same rule, since the release door is where every check must hold.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 21; Appendix G", aliases=("MX hangar", "loose in the bay"), ch=(21,))
g("DO IT RIGHT rules", "course", "DO IT RIGHT is the rule set gzkit adopted after the ten-deep chain of May 24, 2026. No summary is a fact until its source is read. Every delegated result states what it read and what it ran. A subagent's report is T3 until a human or a test confirms it.", "", "The rules answer summary laundering, the failure in which a subagent's summary is passed upward as fact.","Course Topic Guide, Appendix G", aliases=("ten-deep chain",), ch=(18,))
g("Canary", "method", "The course uses the term in two ways. In E5 a canary is a harmless payload planted in a tool description to show that the model follows hidden instructions (the Invariant Labs demonstration). From E11 on it is a planted fake secret string that the builder searches for in images, logs, and commits to prove that a leak path exists, and E12's release gate greps for it.", "The term comes from the canaries that miners carried to detect gas.", "A canary gives a leak test an observable, which is what an attack card requires.","Course Topic Guide, Chapters 14, 20, 21", aliases=("canary string", "canary payload"), ch=(14, 20, 21))
g("Pre-registration", "method", "Pre-registration is the practice of writing the hypothesis, the measure, and the decision rule before collecting the data, so that the result cannot be fitted to the outcome. HARKing (hypothesizing after the results are known) is the failure it prevents.", "Nosek et al., 'The preregistration revolution' (PNAS, 2018); Kerr, 'HARKing' (1998).", "The flight card is pre-registration applied to a build, and E10's gate is pre-registered: the delta that turns it red is written down before the run.","https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1708274114", aliases=("pre-registered", "HARKing", "Nosek", "Kerr"), ch=(19, 23))
g("Attack card", "course", "An attack card is E11's pre-registered adversarial test. It states the objective (which is the falsifier), the failure-mode number it targets, the delivery path, the payload's SHA-256, the guardrail under test, and the expected observable. The Chase writes two of the five cards and seals them, so the builder cannot tune the system to them.", "", "An attack card does for an adversarial test what a flight card does for a build: the falsifier is written before the run.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 20", aliases=("attack cards", "sealed card"), ch=(20,))
g("Two-stage review", "course", "Two-stage review puts two reviewers with different questions in a fixed order, and neither may edit. The spec reviewer asks whether the change does what the brief asks and stays inside the allowed paths. The quality reviewer asks, only after a spec PASS, whether the change is done well. gzkit's four personas are implementer, spec reviewer, quality reviewer, and narrator.", "", "gzkit fixes the order because a quality review of the wrong feature returns a confident PASS.","E8 lecture, slide 8", aliases=("spec reviewer", "quality reviewer", "narrator", "spec review", "quality review"), ch=(17,))
g("Dispatch record", "course", "A dispatch record is gzkit's one record per role per task. It says who was dispatched, with what brief, and what came back. If there is no record, the role did not run, whatever the summary says: 'credit is never inferred.' A run with one agent declares itself with --single-driver --reason.", "gzkit ADR-0.18.0", "E8 decides which roles ran from the dispatch records rather than from the summary.","https://gzkit.org/user/concepts/subagent-pipeline/", aliases=("dispatch records", "credit is never inferred", "single-driver"), ch=(17,))
g("Deployment vocabulary", "method", "E12 introduces these terms. FastAPI is the Python web framework the reference service runs on. A wheel is the binary package format that Python builds. Docker and Podman are container runtimes, and an image digest is the SHA-256 that identifies an exact image, where a tag can move. An idempotency key is a client-supplied id that lets a retried request be applied once. A runbook is the written procedure an operator follows for a release or an incident.", "", "E12's release gate and runbook are written in these terms.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 21", aliases=("FastAPI", "wheel", "Docker", "Podman", "image digest", "idempotency key", "runbook"), ch=(21,))
g("Stability patterns (Nygard)", "paper", "Nygard's Release It! collects stability patterns for production systems. The ones the course uses are Timeouts (never wait forever), Circuit Breaker (stop calling a failing dependency and let it recover), Steady State (a system must run indefinitely without human intervention), Fail Fast (reject early rather than consume resources), Governor (slow down automated actions so a human can intervene), and Force Multiplier (the dark side of automation, which amplifies a mistake across the fleet).", "Nygard, Release It! 2nd ed. (2018).", "E12 applies Governor and Force Multiplier to agents.","https://pragprog.com/titles/mnee2/release-it-second-edition/", aliases=("Timeouts", "Steady State", "Fail Fast", "Governor", "Force Multiplier"), ch=(21,))
g("Toolkit: uv, ruff, ty", "harness", "The course's Python toolkit comes from Astral. uv is the package and environment manager that every command runs through (uv run, uv python install 3.14). ruff is the linter and formatter, and ty is the type checker from the same team. VS Code is the assumed editor, and the GitHub Student Developer Pack supplies the subscriptions Route A uses.", "Astral (uv, ruff, ty).", "Every command in the exercises runs through uv.","https://docs.astral.sh/uv/", aliases=("uv", "ruff", "ty", "Student Developer Pack"))
g("Google ADK", "system", "Google's Agent Development Kit (2025) is a Python and Java framework with workflow agents (sequential, parallel, loop) and LLM agents, built to speak A2A natively.", "Google, 2025.", "The comparison tables in E0 and E2 include it.","https://google.github.io/adk-docs/", aliases=("Agent Development Kit", "ADK"), ch=(11,))
g("Spec-driven kits: spec-kit, OpenSpec, BMAD, GSD", "system", "Spec-driven kits put a written specification ahead of agent-generated code. The four the course compares are GitHub's spec-kit (2025), OpenSpec, BMAD (Breakthrough Method of Agile AI-Driven Development), and GSD.", "", "The orientation's comparison table and Chapter 8's 'spec-driven kits' section place gzkit among them: it shares lineage with spec-kit and adds the ledger, gates, and attestation that the others leave to the human.","https://github.com/github/spec-kit; https://gzkit.org/examples/comparison-gzkit-speckit-bmad/", aliases=("spec-kit", "OpenSpec", "BMAD", "GSD"), ch=(8,))
g("Computer use", "harness", "Computer use is the capability Anthropic released in October 2024 in which the screen is the tool. Perception is a screenshot, and every action (click, type, scroll) is write-class.", "Anthropic, October 2024.", "E3 uses it as the limiting case of a tool contract, since the schema is 'any pixel'.","https://www.anthropic.com/news/3-5-models-and-computer-use", aliases=("computer-use",), ch=(7, 12))
g("MAST categories FC1–FC3", "paper", "Cemri et al.'s taxonomy of multi-agent failures has three categories. FC1 is specification and system design (the task, role, or stopping condition was wrong). FC2 is inter-agent misalignment (agents talked past each other). FC3 is task verification and termination (nobody checked, or checked wrong). The paper reports prevalence by category from annotated traces.", "Cemri et al. (2025), v3.", "The course reads summary laundering as FC2 and FC3 at once.","https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.13657", aliases=("FC1", "FC2", "FC3"), ch=(18,))
g("AI_LOG.md and LEDGER.md", "course", "Every course repository keeps two files. AI_LOG.md is the Sprint's attribution record of what was asked, which model answered, what was kept, what was rejected, why, and how it was verified. LEDGER.md (or ledger.jsonl) is the event log of sorties, verdicts, gates, and commits. The design-analyses/ directory holds the Design Analyses.", "", "The AI log is discussed in Drift, and the ledger is what the Chase reads.","Course Topic Guide, Chapters 22 and 23", aliases=("AI_LOG.md", "LEDGER.md", "AI log", "design-analyses/"), ch=(22, 23))
g("Course Topic Guide", "course", "The Course Topic Guide is the outline edition of the textbook, Answerable Systems. It has twenty-six chapters (Part I the posture, Part II the survey, Part III the build, Part IV the method) and appendices A–G.", "", "When a slide says 'chapter 16' or 'Appendix C', this is the book it means, and the chapter-to-Evolution map on its front page says which chapters each Evolution uses.","/textbook/", aliases=("textbook", "the book"), ch=())
g("OpenTelemetry", "protocol", "OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral standard for traces, metrics, and logs. A trace is a tree of spans, each with a start, an end, attributes, and a parent. OTLP is the wire protocol, and the GenAI semantic conventions define the attributes for model calls and tool calls. Langfuse and LangSmith ingest it.", "CNCF, 2019 onward.", "E11's receipts are spans, and Figure 12 is a span waterfall.","https://opentelemetry.io", aliases=("OTel", "OTLP", "span tree", "GenAI conventions"), ch=(20,))
g("MCP threat mechanisms", "protocol", "E5 covers four ways an MCP server can attack the model that calls it. Tool poisoning hides instructions in a tool description. The confused deputy is a server that acts on the user's authority for someone else's request. A rug pull is a tool description that changes after approval. Cross-server shadowing is one server's description instructing the model about another server's tools.", "Invariant Labs (April 2025) and the MCP security best-practices page.", "The defenses the course builds are hash-pinned descriptions (tool-pins.json), scoped tokens, and the rule that every description is untrusted content.","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/draft/basic/security_best_practices", aliases=("confused deputy", "rug pull", "cross-server shadowing", "resource injection", "tool-pins.json"), ch=(14,))
g("Hollow test (hollow antibody)", "course", "A hollow test is a check that cannot fail. Examples are a test that compares the code with itself, an assertion with no input that could make it false, and a gate that looks for a file rather than a result. A live negative control (remove the feature and watch the test go red) is the cure.", "gzkit's June 2026 audit found that its own hollow-test detector was hollow (ADR-0.0.73).", "E10's Sortie 10 asks for the RED witness for this reason.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 19", aliases=("hollow antibody", "hollow test", "tautological test", "live negative control", "@enforces"), ch=(19,))
g("τ-bench family", "bench", "τ-bench (Sierra, 2024) scores an agent on tool use with a simulated user in retail and airline domains. It introduced pass^k, the chance that k independent trials all succeed, which punishes inconsistency. τ²-bench (2025) adds a user who also acts on tools. AgentBench, CAR-bench, and FieldWorkArena are the other agent benchmarks E10 compares.", "Yao, Shinn, Razavi & Narasimhan (2024).", "E10 uses pass^k because one run is an anecdote and pass^k makes consistency the score.","https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.12045", aliases=("τ-bench", "tau-bench", "pass^k", "τ²-bench", "AgentBench"), ch=(19,))
g("Context rot and lost in the middle", "paper", "Two results concern long context windows. Liu et al. (2023), in 'Lost in the Middle', found that models use information at the start and end of a long context better than information in the middle. Chroma's 2025 'Context Rot' report found that performance degrades as input length grows, even on simple tasks.", "Liu et al. (2023); Chroma (2025).", "Both support E6's budget rule and the four compaction techniques (context editing, tool-result clearing, a keep list, re-seeding).","https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.03172", aliases=("Context Rot", "Lost in the Middle", "context editing", "tool result clearing"), ch=(15,))
g("AI governance frameworks", "org", "E0 situates the course against four bodies of regulation and standards: the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (2023), the EU AI Act (2024, in force in stages), ISO/IEC 42001 (the AI management-system standard, 2023), and the third-party audit literature (Raji et al., 'Outsider Oversight', 2022; Groves et al., 'Auditing Work', 2024).", "", "E0 argues that none of them reaches the engineer's desk and that index zero does.","https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework", aliases=("NIST AI RMF", "EU AI Act", "ISO/IEC 42001", "Raji", "Auditing Work"), ch=(3,))
g("Background and cloud agents", "harness", "Background and cloud agents run without a terminal open. The examples the course surveys are Codex cloud (sandboxed tasks that open pull requests), GitHub Copilot's cloud agent, Claude Code's cloud sessions and GitHub Action, and scheduled routines.", "", "E12's survey treats them as the same loop with the human further from index zero, which is why the release gates matter more for them.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 9", aliases=("background agents", "cloud agent", "Codex cloud agent", "Claude Code GitHub Action", "routines"), ch=(9, 21))
g("Memory retrieval vocabulary", "method", "E7 introduces these terms. Bag-of-words and TF-IDF score a match by word overlap, which is auditable and blind to synonyms (recall.py's Path B). Embeddings and cosine similarity give each text a vector and score by the angle between vectors, which handles paraphrase and is not auditable (Path A, through Ollama's /api/embed with embeddinggemma, nomic-embed-text, or qwen3-embedding). A supersedes field is a record's pointer to the record it replaces. The memory kinds are episodic (what happened), semantic (what is true), procedural (how to), and working state (this session).", "", "E7's build chooses between Path A and Path B, and the choice trades auditability against recall of paraphrase.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 16", aliases=("TF-IDF", "bag-of-words", "cosine", "supersedes", "episodic", "semantic memory", "procedural memory", "nomic-embed-text", "all-minilm"), ch=(16,))
g("Harness configuration knobs", "harness", "E5 and E6 compare the settings each harness exposes: Claude Code's /compact, /context, model_auto_compact_token_limit, and CLAUDE.local.md; OpenCode's compaction settings and its opencode export and stats commands; Cursor's .cursor/rules .mdc files with 'Apply Intelligently'; and Gemini CLI's GEMINI.md and context.fileName.", "", "The control surface differs by harness, and the control-surface matrix records which rules each harness enforces and which it only requests.","https://gzkit.org/governance/agents-md-doctrine/", aliases=("control-surface matrix", "/compact", "/context", "CLAUDE.local.md", "GEMINI.md", "agents-md doctrine"), ch=(14, 15))
g("Routing and model families", "method", "E9 builds cost-aware routing. RouteLLM and FrugalGPT route to the cheap model first and escalate on doubt. A route table carries a budget, and degraded mode is what the system does when the preferred route is down. The open-weight families the decks list as adversaries from another family are GLM, DeepSeek, Kimi, Qwen, MiniMax, MiMo, and Nemotron. A stealth model is one whose family is undisclosed (Big Pickle).", "RouteLLM (Ong et al., 2024); FrugalGPT (Chen, Zaharia & Zou, 2023).", "The second-family adversary that E11's red team uses is drawn from this list.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 18", aliases=("RouteLLM", "FrugalGPT", "degraded mode", "stealth model", "DeepSeek", "Qwen", "Kimi", "GLM", "Nemotron"), ch=(18,))
g("gz commands", "course", "The decks cite these gzkit CLI verbs: gz init, gz prd, gz adr (and gz adr evaluate --red-team), gz validate (with --distribution, --instructions-files-budget, --invariant-coherence), gz handoff, gz insights remember (the corpus of operator corrections), gz obpi dispatch / lock / repudiate / complete, gz covers, gz arb red, gz patch release, gz closeout --ceremony, and gz status.", "", "gzkit is the course's reference design, and these are the commands by which its gates, ledger, and dispatch records are operated.","https://gzkit.org/user/quickstart/", aliases=("gz validate", "gz handoff", "gz obpi", "gz closeout", "gz insights", "insights corpus"), ch=(25,))
g("ADR-1 apparatus", "course", "E2 supplies these tools for writing the first ADR. WWHTBT asks what would have to be true. The pre-mortem assumes the design failed and asks why. Constraint archaeology asks where each constraint came from. The ten peer challenges, the decomposition scorecard, and the abstraction tax (what each layer costs on every call) complete the set.", "", "E2 applies them to ADR-1 so that the first design decision is written down with its reasons before any code exists.","Course Topic Guide, Chapter 11", aliases=("WWHTBT", "pre-mortem", "constraint archaeology", "abstraction tax", "decomposition scorecard"), ch=(11,))
g("gzkit founding documents", "course", "gzkit's founding documents are why.md (the Degradation Pattern, the reason the kit exists), the charter (what the kit promises), the lineage (where it came from), the genesis (the origin transcript), the command doctrine (how commands may act), the control-surface matrix, and the guides (guide-prd, guide-adr, guide-tasks, guide-releasing).", "", "The orientation lists them as readings, and each one is linked from the textbook's Readings.","https://gzkit.org/user/reference/", aliases=("why.md", "genesis.md", "charter", "lineage", "command doctrine"), ch=(3,))
g("Sprint and Drift essays", "course", "Sprint and Drift is the newsletter whose essays open the chapters. The essays are Tree Hollow, Who answers for the code, Enthusiasm Is All You Need, Get the Balance Right, Stop the presses, hail the autoloom, The cost of the wrong abstraction went to zero, Woven, Code ergo sum, Optimism's flames, Newtonian motion, Can you outrun the truth?, and Turn and Face the Change.", "sprintanddrift.substack.com (June–July 2026).", "Each chapter's Readings links the essay it opens with.","https://sprintanddrift.substack.com", aliases=("Who answers for the code", "Newtonian motion", "Turn and Face the Change", "Code ergo sum", "Woven", "Optimism's flames"), ch=(22,))
g("JADE, Voyager, OpenHands, Aider, Jules, Zed", "system", "These are entries in the survey tables. JADE is the Java FIPA-compliant agent platform of the 2000s. Voyager (2023) is a Minecraft agent that writes and stores its skills as code. OpenHands (2024) is an open-source coding-agent platform. Aider (2023) is a terminal pair-programming tool. Jules (2025) is Google's asynchronous coding agent. Zed is an editor with agent features.", "", "The survey tables in Chapters 7 and 8 place each one in the lineage of agent platforms.","", aliases=("JADE", "Voyager", "OpenHands", "Aider", "Jules", "Zed"), ch=(7, 8))
